  "sourceType": "CLASSIC",
  "sourceNotes": "Crostata di visciole is central to the Jewish-Roman kitchen and to the memory of the Roman Jewish Ghetto, where the Forno del Ghetto bakery near Via del Portico d'Ottavia has sold it for generations. It is documented in references including Donatella Pavoncello's Dal 1880 ad oggi: La cucina ebraica della mia famiglia (1982) and more recently in Rachel Roddy's An A-Z of Pasta (2021, mentioned in the context of Roman cooking traditions). The combination of ricotta and sour cherry in a crostata shell is ancient.",
